      <h2>What changes on the desktop</h2>
      <p>
        On the desktop, the browser differences in threads, workers and
        storage mostly go away. Both apps are ordinary Rust programs that use
        every core, draw with <code>wgpu</code> on Metal and read your
        folders in place. What remains is the difference that matters most
        for a photo app: where the pixels come from.
      </p>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>LightPhotos uses ImageIO for everything.</strong> Every
          JPEG, HEIC and RAW file goes through Apple's ImageIO, the library
          that Preview and Photos use. The Mac build does not even compile in
          the RAW library that the other builds use. (See <a
          href="/blogs/what-imageio-gives-you-free.html">What ImageIO gives
          you free</a>.)
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>LightCraft brings its own decoders.</strong> Its own RAW
          decoder and demosaic, and Rust libraries for JPEG, PNG, TIFF, WebP
          and JPEG XL, run on every platform. It does not depend on what
          Apple supports.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>LightCraft has far more finished features.</strong>
          LightPhotos is for going through a folder fast, keeping the good
          photos and giving them quick edits. LightCraft is already a full
          Lightroom-style editor, with masks, tone curves, colour grading,
          healing, lens corrections, presets, versions, history, HDR and
          panorama merges, albums, stacks and export to six formats. Keep
          that in mind with every number below.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="lp-compare-pick">LightPhotos</th>
              <th scope="col">LightCraft</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Decoding</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Apple's ImageIO, for every format</td>
              <td>Its own, in Rust, for every format</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">A RAW at fit-to-window</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">The embedded JPEG, then ImageIO's develop at the window's size</td>
              <td>Its own develop, up to 2560&nbsp;px</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">The photos on either side</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Embedded JPEG ready</td>
              <td>Fully developed</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">HEIC</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Yes</td>
              <td>Not yet</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Canon CR3</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">Yes</td>
              <td>Embedded preview only</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Your photos</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">A folder, read in place, with the thumbnail cache beside the photos</td>
              <td>A library catalog, files stay in place unless you ask to copy them</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">License</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">MIT or Apache-2.0, with no copyleft libraries in the Mac app</td>
              <td>MIT or Apache-2.0</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Import is still a big difference</h2>
      <p>
        On the desktop, both apps can leave your files where they are. The
        difference is in what each app remembers about them.
      </p>
      <p>
        LightCraft keeps a library, the way Lightroom does. A
        <code>LightCraft Library</code> folder holds the catalog, presets,
        thumbnail cache and, if you ask for them, copies of your photos. The
        import dialog scans a folder and its subfolders, skips files that are
        already in the catalog by their content, and reads any XMP sidecars.
        Then it adds each photo in place, copies it into
        <code>Originals/YYYY/YYYY-MM-DD/</code>, or moves it there. Even
        <strong>Browse Folder…</strong>, which looks at a folder without
        importing it, records what it sees in the catalog, and forgets those
        records later if you did not change them. One catalog knows every
        photo, so searches, albums, smart albums and stacks can span all of
        them.
      </p>
      <p>
        LightPhotos deliberately has no catalog. There is no list of your
        folders and no import step. You open a folder, and everything the app
        knows about it is in the <code>.lightphotos</code> folder inside it.
        I tried a central catalog first, and it lost track of ratings every
        time someone moved a folder to another drive (see <a
        href="/blogs/where-your-data-lives.html">where your data lives</a>).
        With nothing central, there is nothing to get out of step with the
        disk. You can move, copy, rename or delete a folder in Finder, and
        the app does not need to know.
      </p>
      <p>
        The cost is that LightPhotos sees one folder at a time. It cannot
        search across a year of shoots or put photos from two folders in one
        album. The next step is to let you open several folders at once,
        not to add a catalog. Each folder will still own its own data, and
        the app will only remember which folders you have open.
      </p>

      <h2>How I measured</h2>
      <p>
        I built both apps with each project's own release script and ran
        them on the same M4 Pro Mac with 48&nbsp;GB of memory, on macOS
        26.3. The photos were the same twenty Sony A7 II RAW files, copied
        to a new folder for every run so that no cache had seen them. Each
        app got a fresh home folder, with default settings and a 1600 by
        1000 point window.
      </p>
      <p>
        As in the browser, a script launches the app, double-clicks the first photo and presses the
        right arrow eight times. It captures the window every 150&nbsp;ms. It
        records the first capture that changed (the app reacted) and the
        point where nothing changed for three seconds (it finished). Each
        number is the median of five runs. The runs agreed to within a few
        percent, except one LightCraft open that took 0.86&nbsp;s. A capture
        takes about 0.23&nbsp;s, so no measurement can be below about
        0.35&nbsp;s. The script, <code>tools/desktop-bench</code>, only looks
        at the window, so it works on any Mac app.
      </p>

      <h2>The numbers</h2>
      <div class="lp-compare-wrap">
        <table class="lp-compare">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col"></th>
              <th scope="col" class="lp-compare-pick">LightPhotos</th>
              <th scope="col">LightCraft</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Download (.dmg)</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">7.3&nbsp;MB</td>
              <td>15.2&nbsp;MB</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">App, installed</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">14&nbsp;MB</td>
              <td>43&nbsp;MB</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Launch to window</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">0.31&nbsp;s</td>
              <td>0.43&nbsp;s</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Launch to finished grid, 20 RAWs</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">0.73&nbsp;s</td>
              <td>1.24&nbsp;s</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Open the first photo: reacts / finished</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">0.42 / 1.12&nbsp;s</td>
              <td>0.48 / 0.51&nbsp;s</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Next photo, each (median of 8): reacts / finished</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">0.35 / 1.11&nbsp;s</td>
              <td>0.37 / 0.38&nbsp;s</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">Memory afterwards</th>
              <td class="lp-compare-pick">0.9&nbsp;GB</td>
              <td>1.7&nbsp;GB</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        One note on the grid. LightCraft opens its grid scrolled to the end.
        At this window size, 11 of the 20 thumbnails were on screen, so its
        1.24&nbsp;s is for those 11. LightPhotos showed all 20.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the next photo reversed</h2>
      <p>
        In the browser, LightPhotos finished the next photo in 0.53&nbsp;s,
        and LightCraft in 1.88&nbsp;s. The timing logs in both apps show why
        the Mac reverses this. LightPhotos shows the camera's embedded JPEG
        within about 20&nbsp;ms, so both apps react at the measurement floor.
        But the A7 II's embedded JPEG is only 1616&nbsp;px wide, and the
        photo area of this window on a Retina screen is 3584&nbsp;px wide. So when you arrive, LightPhotos asks ImageIO to
        develop the RAW data at that size, which took 0.5 to 0.6&nbsp;s.
      </p>
      <p>
        LightCraft develops at up to 2560&nbsp;px, about half as many
        pixels, and it decodes and develops both neighbours in the
        background and keeps them ready. So the arrow key costs only a GPU
        pass of about 35&nbsp;ms. My script waits a few seconds on each
        photo, which gives LightCraft time to get ahead. That is about the
        pace of a person who looks at each photo. If
        you hold the arrow key down, you could move faster than LightCraft
        can prepare. I did not measure that.
      </p>
      <p>
        I do not think either is the wrong choice. LightCraft spends CPU
        time and battery on develops that you never look at. LightPhotos has
        something on screen just as fast, and the camera's JPEG is plenty to
        judge framing and expression, which is most of culling. In the half
        second after that, the photo sharpens to full Retina detail while
        you already look at it.
      </p>
      <p>
        LightPhotos could prepare its neighbours too, and it would cost less
        memory than for LightCraft, because LightPhotos keeps finished 8-bit
        images: about 34&nbsp;MB each at this size, so about 70&nbsp;MB for
        two. Whether that is worth
        the wasted develops depends on how people move through a folder. I
        want to measure that first.
      </p>

      <h2>Why the grid and the memory go the other way</h2>
      <p>
        For a grid thumbnail, LightPhotos asks ImageIO for an image no bigger
        than the thumbnail, and ImageIO answers from the embedded JPEG in 12 to 22&nbsp;ms per
        file. So twenty RAW files fill the grid in under a second.
        LightCraft also shows the embedded JPEGs first, but then replaces
        them with thumbnails that it develops from the RAW data, and its grid
        is not finished until those arrive.
      </p>
      <p>
        For memory, LightCraft keeps the decoded RAW data for the current
        photo and both neighbours as 32-bit floating-point RGB, at 12 bytes
        a pixel. That is about 52&nbsp;MB per photo at 2560&nbsp;px. This
        data lets its sliders use the full range of the sensor: highlights
        above white, and exposure changes without banding. LightPhotos gets
        finished 8-bit images from ImageIO, at 4 bytes a pixel, and keeps
        only small embedded JPEGs for the neighbours. On screen, the two look
        the same. Those sources explain only about 150&nbsp;MB of the
        0.8&nbsp;GB difference. I did not break down the rest.
      </p>
      <p>
        The app size is ImageIO again. LightCraft ships its own decoders for
        RAW, JPEG, JPEG XL and more, plus its own colour management, and its release build skips link-time
        optimisation and keeps some debug information. The macOS decoders
        cost LightPhotos nothing.
      </p>

      <h2>What ImageIO costs</h2>
      <p>
        ImageIO is not free in every sense. The photos look the way Apple
        develops them, which is not the same as LightPhotos on Linux, on
        Windows or in the browser. LightPhotos supports only the cameras
        that Apple supports, when Apple supports them. When ImageIO decodes
        a file incorrectly, I cannot fix it.
      </p>
      <p>
        LightCraft gets the opposite trade. One decoder and one develop
        everywhere means the same photo looks the same on every platform,
        and LightCraft can fix any bug in its decoder. The cost is a bigger
        app and the formats it has not written yet. HEIC needs an HEVC decoder, and no
        pure-Rust one with a permissive license exists yet. CR3 files show
        only their embedded preview for now.
      </p>

      <h2>Wrapping up</h2>
      <p>
        On the Mac, LightPhotos relies on the operating system, and
        LightCraft relies on itself. LightPhotos is smaller, starts faster,
        fills its grid faster and uses less memory, because Apple already
        did that work, and because a much smaller feature set fits in a
        smaller app. LightCraft is faster where it chose to do more work ahead of time, looks
        the same everywhere and does far more when you start to edit. Both
        feel fast when you step through a shoot, for different reasons.
      </p>

      <nav class="lp-article-nav" aria-label="More posts">
        <a class="lp-article-nav-prev" href="/blogs/two-rust-photo-apps-in-the-browser.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Back</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">Two Rust photo apps in the browser</span>
        </a>
        <a class="lp-article-nav-next" href="/blogs/linux-raw-photo-editor.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Next</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">A fast RAW photo editor for Linux</span>
        </a>
      </nav>
    </article>
  </main>
  <script>
    // Steps through a row of nine photos. LightCraft develops both neighbours
    // of the photo on screen; LightPhotos develops only the photo on screen.
    const fig = document.getElementById("step-demo");
    if (fig) {
      const N = 9;
      const START = 1;
      // Seconds for one step, from the table: [reacts, finished].
      const TIMES = { lp: [0.35, 1.11], lc: [0.37, 0.38] } as const;
      const PX_PER_S = 516 / 1.2;
      const NEAR = { lp: "JPEG", lc: "ready" } as const;
      type App = keyof typeof TIMES;

      const q = (sel: string) => fig.querySelector(sel) as SVGElement;
      const el = (name: string) => q(`[data-el="${name}"]`);
      const readout = fig.querySelector(".lp-try-readout") as HTMLElement;
      const nextBtn = fig.querySelector('[data-act="next"]') as HTMLButtonElement;
      const reduce = window.matchMedia("(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)");

      let cur: number, dropped: number[], lcDev: number, lcUnused: number, lpDev: number, frame = 0;

      const neighbours = (i: number) => [i - 1, i + 1].filter((n) => n >= 0 && n < N);
      const plural = (n: number, word: string) => `${n} ${word}${n === 1 ? "" : "s"}`;

      function cell(app: App, i: number) {
        let cls = "lp-fig-panel", text = "", textCls = "lp-fig-s";
        if (i === cur) { cls = "lp-fig-box-accent"; text = "now"; textCls = "lp-fig-t"; }
        else if (neighbours(cur).includes(i)) { cls = "lp-fig-box"; text = NEAR[app]; }
        else if (app === "lc" && dropped.includes(i)) { cls = "lp-fig-bad-stroke"; text = "unused"; }
        q(`[data-cell="${app}${i}"]`).setAttribute("class", cls);
        const label = q(`[data-label="${app}${i}"]`);
        label.setAttribute("class", textCls);
        label.textContent = text;
      }

      function bars(app: App, t: number | null) {
        const [react, finish] = TIMES[app];
        const r = t === null ? 0 : Math.min(t, react);
        const f = t === null ? 0 : Math.max(0, Math.min(t, finish) - react);
        el(`${app}-react`).setAttribute("width", String(r * PX_PER_S));
        el(`${app}-finish`).setAttribute("x", String(react * PX_PER_S));
        el(`${app}-finish`).setAttribute("width", String(f * PX_PER_S));
        el(`${app}-time`).textContent =
          t === null ? "not timed" : t >= finish ? `${react.toFixed(2)} / ${finish.toFixed(2)} s` : `${Math.min(t, finish).toFixed(2)} s`;
      }

      function render() {
        for (let i = 0; i < N; i++) { cell("lp", i); cell("lc", i); }
        el("lp-dev").textContent = plural(lpDev, "develop");
        el("lp-unused").textContent = "0 unused";
        el("lc-dev").textContent = plural(lcDev, "develop");
        el("lc-unused").textContent = `${lcUnused} unused`;
        nextBtn.disabled = cur === N - 1;
      }

      const counts = () =>
        `LightCraft: ${plural(lcDev, "develop")}, ${lcUnused} unused. LightPhotos: ${plural(lpDev, "develop")}, 0 unused.`;

      function moveTo(target: number) {
        cancelAnimationFrame(frame);
        const ready = neighbours(cur);
        const wasReady = ready.includes(target);
        dropped = ready.filter((n) => n !== target);
        lcUnused += dropped.length;
        lcDev += (wasReady ? 0 : 1) + neighbours(target).filter((n) => n !== cur).length;
        lpDev += 1;
        cur = target;
        render();
        return wasReady;
      }

      function step() {
        if (cur >= N - 1) return;
        moveTo(cur + 1);
        const done = () => {
          bars("lp", 9); bars("lc", 9);
          readout.textContent = `Photo ${cur + 1}: both react in about 0.35 s. LightCraft is finished at 0.38 s, LightPhotos at 1.11 s. ${counts()}`;
        };
        if (reduce.matches) return done();
        const start = performance.now();
        const tick = (now: number) => {
          const t = Math.max(0, now - start) / 1000;
          bars("lp", t); bars("lc", t);
          if (t < TIMES.lp[1]) frame = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
          else done();
        };
        frame = requestAnimationFrame(tick);
      }

      function jump() {
        moveTo((cur + 4) % N);
        bars("lp", null); bars("lc", null);
        readout.textContent = `Jump to photo ${cur + 1}: neither app had developed it. I did not time this case. ${counts()}`;
      }

      function reset() {
        cancelAnimationFrame(frame);
        cur = START; dropped = []; lcDev = 3; lcUnused = 0; lpDev = 1;
        render();
        bars("lp", 9); bars("lc", 9);
        readout.textContent = "Photo 2 of 9. Press Next photo to step, or Jump in the grid to skip ahead.";
      }

      reset();
      nextBtn.addEventListener("click", step);
      fig.querySelector('[data-act="jump"]')!.addEventListener("click", jump);
      fig.querySelector('[data-act="reset"]')!.addEventListener("click", reset);
    }
  </script>
</Base>
